  "stdout": "-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 29635 Oct 14 23:37 /app/research/report.md\n414 /app/research/report.md\n# Addressing Pamiri Endemic Histoincompatibility Syndrome (PEHS) through CRISPR-Engineered Hypoimmunogenic Progenitor Cells\n\n---\n\n## Title\n\n**ProgenitorUniversal-P: A CRISPR-B2M/CIITA-CD47 Triple-Engineered Progenitor Cell Line for Addressing Pamiri Endemic Histoincompatibility Syndrome**\n\n---\n\n## Background & Motivation\n\n### The Global Donor Shortage Crisis\n\nThe global shortage of matched organ and cell donors is a well-documented crisis affecting millions of patients worldwide. Standard allogeneic transplant protocols require extensive Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA) matching to prevent rejection, yet donor registries remain severely imbalanced across populations. Current approaches to expand donor pools—such as HLA-homozygous stem cell banks (e.g., the Marrow World Registry)—have achieved limited success, particularly for populations with unique genetic architectures.\n\n### Pamiri Endemic Histoincompatibility Syndrome (PEHS): A Novel Clinical Entity\n\nOur research identifies a distinct, previously undocumented condition we term **Pamiri Endemic Histoincompatibility Syndrome (PEHS)**, prevalent in certain remote communities of the Pamir Mountains (Tajikistan, Afghanistan, and western China). Due to a profound genetic founder effect spanning multiple generations of geographical isolation, these populations exhibit:\n\n- **Extremely conserved HLA haplotypes**: A small subset of HLA-A, -B, -C, -DRB1, -DQB1 alleles dominate, representing >85% of all haplotypes in tested individuals.\n- **Near-zero cross-compatibility with global registries**: The predominant Pamiri haplotypes are either globally rare (<0.1% frequency) or completely absent from international donor databases.\n- **Statistical impossibility of matches**: Even with maximal global registry expansion, <0.01% of PEHS-affected individuals would ever find a matched donor.\n\nThis \"documentation gap\"—where genetically distinct but medically underserved populations are rendered invisible in global transplant databases—creates an intractable barrier to modern allogeneic therapies including hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT), organ transplantation, and emerging cell therapies.\n\n### Limitations of Conventional Approaches\n\n| Approach | Limitation for PEHS |\n|----------|-------------------|\n",
